e="width:2.5px;height:2px;margin:0 1.5px;border-radius:50%;background:rgb(110, 110, 110);flex-shrink:0;transition:height 60ms linear, background 80ms linear;will-change:transform, height"></div><div style="width:2.5px;height:2px;margin:0 1.5px;border-radius:50%;background:rgb(110, 110, 110);flex-shrink:0;transition:height 60ms linear, background 80ms linear;will-change:transform, height"></div></div><div style="position:absolute;inset:0;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;pointer-events:none;will-change:opacity, transform;opacity:1;transform:scale(1);transition:opacity 280ms cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1), transform 280ms cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1)"><div style="position:relative;width:18px;height:18px;flex-shrink:0"><div style="position:absolute;inset:0;border-radius:50%;overflow:hidden;background:url(/cloudsBlue.webp) 90% 100% / 120% 120% no-repeat;box-shadow:0 2px 6px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.35), 0 0 2px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2), inset 0 2px 3px rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.35), inset 0 -2px 3px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2)"></div><div style="position:absolute;inset:0;border-radius:50%;background:radial-gradient(ellipse 70% 50% at 40% 25%, rgba(255,255,255,0.5) 0%, rgba(255,255,255,0.15) 40%, transparent 70%)"></div><div style="position:absolute;inset:0;border-radius:50%;background:radial-gradient(ellipse 60% 30% at 55% 80%, rgba(200,230,255,0.2) 0%, transparent 70%)"></div><div style="position:absolute;inset:0;border-radius:50%;box-shadow:inset 0 0 4px rgba(210, 235, 255, 0.25), inset 0 1px 1px rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.3), inset 0 -1px 2px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.15);border:0.5px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.25)"></div><img alt="Glass lens highlight on the VoiceOS orb" loading="lazy" decoding="async" data-nimg="fill" style="position:absolute;height:100%;width:100%;left:0;top:0;right:0;bottom:0;color:transparent;inset:0" sizes="32px" srcSet="/_next/image?url=%2Flens.png&amp;w=32&amp;q=75 32w, /_next/image?url=%2Flens.png&amp;w=48&amp;q=75 48w, /_next/image?url=%2Flens.png&amp;w=64&amp;q=75 64w, /_next/image?url=%2Flens.png&amp;w=96&amp;q=75 96w, /_next/image?url=%2Flens.png&amp;w=128&amp;q=75 128w, /_next/image?url=%2Flens.png&amp;w=256&amp;q=75 256w, /_next/image?url=%2Flens.png&amp;w=384&amp;q=75 384w, /_next/image?url=%2Flens.png&amp;w=640&amp;q=75 640w, /_next/image?url=%2Flens.png&amp;w=750&amp;q=75 750w, /_next/image?url=%2Flens.png&amp;w=828&amp;q=75 828w, /_next/image?url=%2Flens.png&amp;w=1080&amp;q=75 1080w, /_next/image?url=%2Flens.png&amp;w=1200&amp;q=75 1200w, /_next/image?url=%2Flens.png&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75 1920w, /_next/image?url=%2Flens.png&amp;w=2048&amp;q=75 2048w, /_next/image?url=%2Flens.png&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75 3840w" src="/_next/image?url=%2Flens.png&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75"/></div></div></div></div><div class="jsx-2593660202 lns-card"><div class="jsx-1197279904 spd-answer"><span class="jsx-1197279904 spd-answer-eyebrow">Suggested reply</span><p class="jsx-1197279904 spd-answer-text"></p></div></div></div></div></div><figcaption class="jsx-1197279904">Hold the Agent Mode trigger, move your cursor over the message you mean, and ask out loud. VoiceOS sees the marked part of your screen and answers in the notch.</figcaption></figure><div class="rounded-2xl p-5 md:p-6 mt-8" style="background-color:#fff;border:1px solid #eee"><h3 style="font-size:clamp(1.125rem, 3vw, 1.375rem);font-weight:500;line-height:1.4;color:#0a0a0a;margin-bottom:1rem">How to point at your screen and ask VoiceOS</h3><ol class="space-y-3"><li class="flex gap-3" style="font-size:1.0625rem;font-weight:400;line-height:1.8;color:#444"><span class="flex h-7 w-7 flex-shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full" style="background-color:#eef6ff;color:#126ff6;font-size:0.8125rem;font-weight:600;margin-top:0.1rem">1</span><span>In the VoiceOS dashboard, open Agent Mode Actions and turn on “Point and ask about your screen.” Grant screen access if your computer asks for permission.</span></li><li class="flex gap-3" style="font-size:1.0625rem;font-weight:400;line-height:1.8;color:#444"><span class="flex h-7 w-7 flex-shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full" style="background-color:#eef6ff;color:#126ff6;font-size:0.8125rem;font-weight:600;margin-top:0.1rem">2</span><span>Open the message, page, form, document, or photo you want help with, then hold your Agent Mode trigger.</span></li><li class="flex gap-3" style="font-size:1.0625rem;font-weight:400;line-height:1.8;color:#444"><span class="flex h-7 w-7 flex-shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full" style="background-color:#eef6ff;color:#126ff6;font-size:0.8125rem;font-weight:600;margin-top:0.1rem">3</span><span>Ask your question while moving the mouse cursor to the exact item. If the screen is crowded, draw around it so the blue trail makes your meaning clear.</span></li><li class="flex gap-3" style="font-size:1.0625rem;font-weight:400;line-height:1.8;color:#444"><span class="flex h-7 w-7 flex-shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full" style="background-color:#eef6ff;color:#126ff6;font-size:0.8125rem;font-weight:600;margin-top:0.1rem">4</span><span>Release the trigger. VoiceOS reads the marked screen together with your spoken question and answers in context.</span></li></ol></div><div class="rounded-2xl p-5 mt-6" style="background-color:#fff;border:1px solid #eee"><p style="font-size:0.9375rem;font-weight:400;line-height:1.8;color:#444;margin:0">Related: <a style="color:#0a0a0a;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:#0a0a0a40;text-underline-offset:2px;font-weight:500" href="/blog/google-ai-pointer-voice-operating-system">Google&#x27;s AI Pointer: Reinventing the Cursor</a> · <a style="color:#0a0a0a;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:#0a0a0a40;text-underline-offset:2px;font-weight:500" href="/blog/stop-sending-screenshots-to-chatgpt">Stop Sending Screenshots to ChatGPT to Ask a Question</a> · <a style="color:#0a0a0a;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:#0a0a0a40;text-underline-offset:2px;font-weight:500" href="/blog/circle-to-search-on-your-computer">Circle to Search, But for Your Whole Computer</a></p></div></section><section class="mb-16" id="seeing-to-doing"><h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 2rem);font-weight:500;line-height:1.3;color:#0a0a0a;letter-spacing:-0.02em;margin-bottom:1.5rem">When seeing your screen turns into doing the work</h2><p style="font-size:1.0625rem;font-weight:400;line-height:1.8;color:#444;margin-bottom:1.5rem">Answering questions is where a screen-aware assistant earns your trust. Taking action is where it changes your day.</p><p style="font-size:1.0625rem;font-weight:400;line-height:1.8;color:#444;margin-bottom:1.5rem">VoiceOS does more than explain what is on your screen. In Dictate mode, it turns natural speech into polished text anywhere your cursor is, cleaning up filler, fixing grammar, and matching the tone of the app you are in. In Edit mode, you reshape text you have already written just by talking to it: make this shorter, turn this into bullet points, make it friendlier. And in Agent mode, seeing your screen becomes doing the work. You can ask VoiceOS to reply to the message you are looking at, summarize a thread and draft a response, or carry a task across connected apps like Gmail, Slack, and Google Calendar.</p><p style="font-size:1.0625rem;font-weight:400;line-height:1.8;color:#444">That is the real leap. A tool that can see your screen and also act on it collapses the distance between &quot;how do I do this?&quot; and &quot;just do this for me.&quot; And because handing your computer more capability should never mean handing over control, VoiceOS prepares important actions and confirms with you before anything is sent. You direct the work. It handles the busywork in between.</p><div class="rounded-2xl p-5 mt-6" style="background-color:#fff;border:1px solid #eee"><p style="font-size:0.9375rem;font-weight:400;line-height:1.8;color:#444;margin:0">Related: <a style="color:#0a0a0a;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:#0a0a0a40;text-underline-offset:2px;font-weight:500" href="/blog/voice-ai-agent-vs-dictation">Beyond Dictation: Why Voice AI Agents Are Replacing Voice Typing</a> · <a style="color:#0a0a0a;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:#0a0a0a40;text-underline-offset:2px;font-weight:500" href="/blog/jarvis-control-your-computer-with-your-voice">Real-Life Jarvis: Control Your Computer With Your Voice</a></p></div></section><section class="mb-16" id="how-to-start"><h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 2rem);font-weight:500;line-height:1.3;color:#0a0a0a;letter-spacing:-0.02em;margin-bottom:1.5rem">How to start</h2><p style="font-size:1.0625rem;font-weight:400;line-height:1.8;color:#444;margin-bottom:1.5rem">You do not have to change how you work overnight. The easiest place to begin is low-risk. The next time you are stuck in an app, instead of opening a new browser tab, just ask about your screen. Get an answer in context, in seconds, without losing your place.</p><p style="font-size:1.0625rem;font-weight:400;line-height:1.8;color:#444;margin-bottom:1.5rem">From there, let it do more. Use voice to reshape a rough draft instead of rewriting it by hand. Ask questions about a document, a dashboard, or an email thread instead of parsing it yourself. Then, when you are comfortable, use Agent Mode for real actions like drafting replies, creating events, and chaining steps across apps, with the confirmation loop keeping you in command.</p><p style="font-size:1.0625rem;font-weight:400;line-height:1.8;color:#444">Keep your keyboard and mouse for the precise work they are still best at. Use a screen-aware voice assistant for intent, for the moments when you know what you want but not where the button is. The goal was never to make your computer more complicated. It was to make it finally understand what you are looking at, and help.</p></section><section class="mb-16" id="faq"><h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 2rem);font-weight:500;line-height:1.3;color:#0a0a0a;letter-spacing:-0.02em;margin-bottom:1.5rem">Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2><div class="space-y-6"><div class="rounded-2xl p-5 md:p-6" style="background-color:#fff;border:1px solid #eee"><h3 style="font-size:clamp(1.125rem, 3vw, 1.375rem);font-weight:500;line-height:1.4;color:#0a0a0a;margin-bottom:0.5rem">What is an AI that reads your screen?</h3><p style="font-size:0.9375rem;font-weight:400;line-height:1.8;color:#444">An AI that reads your screen is a screen-aware, multimodal assistant that uses the visible screen as context. It can recognize text, app layouts, charts, buttons, images, and error messages, then answer questions about what it sees. VoiceOS brings that capability to Mac and Windows and lets you ask by voice instead of uploading screenshots to a separate chat.</p></div><div class="rounded-2xl p-5 md:p-6" style="background-color:#fff;border:1px solid #eee"><h3 style="font-size:clamp(1.125rem, 3vw, 1.375rem);font-weight:500;line-height:1.4;color:#0a0a0a;margin-bottom:0.5rem">Is there an AI that can see my screen and answer questions?</h3><p style="font-size:0.9375rem;font-weight:400;line-height:1.8;color:#444">Yes. VoiceOS can see your current screen and answer questions about it on Mac and Windows. Turn on “Point and ask about your screen,” hold the Agent Mode trigger, and ask about whatever is visible. It is an AI that sees your screen and answers questions based on the real page, app, chart, document, or error in front of you.</p></div><div class="rounded-2xl p-5 md:p-6" style="background-color:#fff;border:1px solid #eee"><h3 style="font-size:clamp(1.125rem, 3vw, 1.375rem);font-weight:500;line-height:1.4;color:#0a0a0a;margin-bottom:0.5rem">How is this different from a chatbot like ChatGPT?</h3><p style="font-size:0.9375rem;font-weight:400;line-height:1.8;color:#444">A standard chatbot lives in its own window and only knows what you type into it, so to get help you have to describe your screen in words. A screen-aware voice assistant closes that gap. It can see your screen with your permission, so it already knows the context, and it works system-wide across your apps instead of inside a single tab. You talk, it looks, it helps, with no copy-pasting and no explaining.</p></div><div class="rounded-2xl p-5 md:p-6" style="background-color:#fff;border:1px solid #eee"><h3 style="font-size:clamp(1.125rem, 3vw, 1.375rem);font-weight:500;line-height:1.4;color:#0a0a0a;margin-bottom:0.5rem">What can an AI that scans your screen do?</h3><p style="font-size:0.9375rem;font-weight:400;line-height:1.8;color:#444">Common uses include learning unfamiliar software without watching a tutorial, getting un-stuck the instant you cannot find a setting, understanding dense dashboards or documents, decoding confusing error messages, and turning &quot;how do I do this?&quot; into an action the assistant performs for you. With VoiceOS you can also dictate and edit text by voice and run multi-step tasks across apps like Gmail, Slack, and Google Calendar.</p></div><div class="rounded-2xl p-5 md:p-6" style="background-color:#fff;border:1px solid #eee"><h3 style="font-size:clamp(1.125rem, 3vw, 1.375rem);font-weight:500;line-height:1.4;color:#0a0a0a;margin-bottom:0.5rem">Is it always watching my screen? What about privacy?</h3><p style="font-size:0.9375rem;font-weight:400;line-height:1.8;color:#444">No. A well-designed screen-aware assistant should look only when you ask it to, not record continuously. VoiceOS looks at your screen on demand, when you invoke it, rather than constantly capturing in the background, and it asks for confirmation before taking important actions. You stay in control of when it sees your screen and what happens next.</p></div><div class="rounded-2xl p-5 md:p-6" style="background-color:#fff;border:1px solid #eee"><h3 style="font-size:clamp(1.125rem, 3vw, 1.375rem);font-weight:500;line-height:1.4;color:#0a0a0a;margin-bottom:0.5rem">What is the best screen-aware voice assistant for Mac and Windows in 2026?</h3><p style="font-size:0.9375rem;font-weight:400;line-height:1.8;color:#444">VoiceOS is one of the best options in 2026 because it is built for regular people, not just developers. It works system-wide on both Mac and Windows, understands natural language, sees your screen when you ask, and needs no setup, API keys, or coding. Backed by Y Combinator, it combines screen-aware questions, dictation, voice editing, and agent actions in a single app you can start using in minutes.</p></div><div class="rounded-2xl p-5 md:p-6" style="background-color:#fff;border:1px solid #eee"><h3 style="font-size:clamp(1.125rem, 3vw, 1.375rem);font-weight:500;line-height:1.4;color:#0a0a0a;margin-bottom:0.5rem">Do I need to be technical to use one?</h3><p style="font-size:0.9375rem;font-weight:400;line-height:1.8;color:#444">No. VoiceOS is designed for everyone, especially people who are new to AI. There is no code to write and no configuration to learn. You download it, grant permission, and start talking to your computer about whatever is on your screen.</p></div><div class="rounded-2xl p-5 md:p-6" style="background-color:#fff;border:1px solid #eee"><h3 style="font-size:clamp(1.125rem, 3vw, 1.375rem);font-weight:500;line-height:1.4;color:#0a0a0a;margin-bottom:0.5rem">Can the assistant understand what I am pointing at with my cursor?</h3><p style="font-size:0.9375rem;font-weight:400;line-height:1.8;color:#444">Yes. With “Point and ask about your screen” enabled, hold your Agent Mode trigger and move the mouse cursor while you speak. VoiceOS draws a blue trail, so you can point to one item or circle a region and ask about “this” or “that.” The marked screen and your words are read together, which is often much faster than describing the location.</p></div></div></section><section class="mb-8"><div class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-[20px] py-14 md:py-20 px-6" style="background-color:#F5F9FF;color:#0a0a0a;border:1px solid #e5edf8"><div class="absolute inset-0" style="background-image:url(/oceanWarp.webp);background-size:cover;background-position:center"></div><div class="absolute inset-0 pointer-events-none" style="background-color:rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.7)"></div><div class="absolute inset-0 pointer-events-none" style="background:radial-gradient(ellipse 120% 120% at 50% 50%, transparent 30%, rgba(255,255,255,0.5) 70%, rgba(255,255,255,0.85) 100%)"></div><div class="relative text-center flex flex-col items-center gap-4"><h2 style="font-size:clamp(1.375rem, 4vw, 1.75rem);font-weight:600;line-height:1.3;letter-spacing:-0.02em;color:#0a0a0a">Ask an AI about anything on your screen</h2><p style="font-size:0.9375rem;color:rgba(10,10,10,0.58)">VoiceOS is an AI that reads your screen and answers questions on Mac and Windows. Point with your cursor, ask by voice, and get help without leaving the app you are in.</p><a class="mt-2 inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-2.5 rounded-full text-sm font-medium border border-black/[0.06] shadow-[0_1px_2px_rgba(0,0,0,0.04),0_4px_12px_rgba(0,0,0,0.06)] transition-all duration-200 hover:-translate-y-[1px] hover:shadow-[0_2px_4px_rgba(0,0,0,0.08),0_8px_20px_rgba(0,0,0,0.08)]" style="background-color:#fff;color:#111" href="/">Download VoiceOS<svg class="w-3.5 h-3.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2.5" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7"></path></svg></a></div></div></section></article></div><aside class="hidden lg:block"><div class="sticky top-32"><div class="flex flex-row lg:flex-col gap-2"><button style="width:36px;height:36px;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid #e5e5e5;background-color:#fff;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;cursor:pointer;transition:border-color 0.2s, background-color 0.2s" title="Share on X" aria-label="Share on X"><svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="#0a0a0a"><path d="M18.244 2.25h3.308l-7.227 8.26 8.502 11.24H16.17l-5.214-6.817L4.99 21.75H1.68l7.73-8.835L1.254 2.25H8.08l4.713 6.231zm-1.161 17.52h1.833L7.084 4.126H5.117z"